#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""orionx-postured — make the threat-posture tier mean something.

@decision DEC-PHASE12-024
@title Threat posture is enforced by a daemon, and a blind IDS says so out loud
@status accepted
@rationale W10-5 shipped the tier selector (DEC-PHASE10-005) and W11-6 Layer A
  shipped Suricata behind a lazy-start gate (DEC-PHASE11-008). Nothing joined
  them. Selecting "Tier 2 · Deception" wrote a '2' to
  ~/.config/orionx/threat-posture, recoloured one Cockpit badge, and changed
  nothing else: `nmap -Pn -A` against the deck at Tier 2 produced no Suricata
  output at all. Three independent reasons, all real:

    1. Nothing started suricata.service when the tier was raised.
    2. Its drop-in sets ConditionPathExists=/var/lib/suricata/orionx-enabled
       and that file never existed, so systemd skipped the unit and reported
       success while doing nothing.
    3. /var/lib/suricata/rules is empty. The 22 files in /etc/suricata/rules
       are Debian's stock decoder/stream protocol-anomaly rules, not threat
       signatures. ET-Open arrives only via `orionx-freshen-suricata`, which
       needs a network the deck may not have.

  This daemon closes all three, and the third one is the reason it exists.
  A detector that is installed, enabled, running, and carrying no signatures
  reports nothing — which is indistinguishable, from the operator's chair,
  from a quiet network. That is the failure mode that made the deck look like
  it was watching when it was not. So: SILENT NON-DETECTION IS A BUG. Whenever
  a tier that implies IDS coverage is active without usable threat rules, this
  daemon says so on the bus, at critical, naming the remedy, and keeps saying
  it. It would rather be annoying than dishonest.

  Design constraints that shaped it:

  - Air-gap first. stdlib only, no pip, no network lookups. The ATT&CK map is
    a static local table; the Nebula call is to 127.0.0.1.
  - The model never gates an alert. Every alert is published BEFORE it is
    queued for contextualisation, on a bounded queue, from a worker thread.
    If ollama is absent, slow, or wedged, detection is unaffected.
  - No second scan detector. orionx-scanwatch (DEC-PHASE12-022) already reports
    port scans from the firewall's own drop log at Tier 0 and needs no rules.
    Suricata's scan-class alerts for a source scanwatch just reported are
    suppressed, so one scan is one story, not two.
  - Deception is local and non-destructive: listening decoys and canary files
    on this host. It never probes, scans, or sends anything to another host.
    Tier 2 is the explicit operator opt-in DEC-PHASE9-008 requires.
  - Runtime state only. The decoy firewall openings live in their own nft
    table created at arm time and destroyed at disarm; /etc/nftables.conf
    remains the single authority for base policy and is never edited.

@decision DEC-PHASE12-028
@title Zeek is ingested if it is there, discovered at runtime, by this daemon
  rather than by a second one
@status accepted
@rationale Zeek is not in Debian 13 main. The build tries to install it from
  the OpenSUSE Build Service (0500 hook, DEC-PHASE12-003/015) and that block
  is SOFT-FAIL, so whether a given ISO has Zeek is decided by whether a repo
  answered during the build. `grep -c zeek iso/chroot.files` on the 2026-09-27
  image returns 0: the shipped deck has no Zeek, while 0700 skips its symlink
  and artifact-analyzer.py still lists zeek as a wrapped tool. An operator can
  also install it post-boot at any time (/opt/orionx/optional/install-zeek.sh).

  So "is Zeek present" is not a build-time fact and must not be treated as
  one. This daemon re-checks on every reassert cycle. Zeek installed an hour
  after boot is ingested an hour after boot, with no restart, no rebuild, and
  no reconfiguration — and the transition is ANNOUNCED, so the operator learns
  their deck just gained a capability.

  Why here and not in a new daemon: this daemon already owns the tier, already
  tails an IDS log, already maps alerts to ATT&CK, already de-duplicates scans
  against orionx-scanwatch, and already writes the Cockpit's status file. A
  second daemon would need its own copy of the tier to decide what to do with
  a notice, and two things holding a tier is two things that drift. Zeek ingest
  is therefore a subordinate reader inside the existing enforcement loop.

  Zeek ingest is NOT tier-gated, and that is deliberate. Reading a log file the
  deck has already written costs nothing and sends nothing, exactly like
  orionx-scanwatch at Tier 0 (DEC-PHASE12-022). Gating it behind Tier 1 would
  mean a Tier 0 deck with Zeek running throws away detections it already has.
  It appears in tier_plan() as a plan key that is True at every tier so that
  `--print-plan` states it outright rather than leaving it implicit.

  What is ingested, and what is deliberately not:
    notice.log — Zeek's own judgements. This is the signal; every notice is a
      candidate event, severity-mapped, ATT&CK-tagged where honest.
    weird.log  — protocol anomalies. Individually near-worthless and extremely
      high volume, so these are AGGREGATED: a weird type only reaches the bus
      when it bursts past a threshold in a window. Publishing them one by one
      would bury the notices that matter.
    conn.log   — NOT alerted on. Connection records are the wrong shape for an
      alert and would flood the bus. They are counted, and the count is used
      for one thing: proving Zeek is not blind. Zeek running with a log
      directory that produces zero connections is the same failure as Suricata
      with no rules, and it gets the same treatment — said out loud.

  Not reporting one scan three times. Three detectors can now see the same
  sweep: orionx-scanwatch from the firewall drop log, Suricata from its rules,
  and Zeek from Scan::Port_Scan. SCAN_REPORTER_PRECEDENCE fixes the order once
  — firewall, then suricata, then zeek — and each reporter suppresses a scan
  source that anything ahead of it already published. The bus itself is the
  shared state, so there is no second place for the three to disagree.

Usage:
    orionx-postured                       # daemon (systemd)
    orionx-postured --once --dry-run      # apply nothing, print the plan
    orionx-postured --print-plan          # JSON plan for the current tier
"""
